|---|---|---|
| **Throughput** | Handles 10k msg/s (our 10× target) comfortably on one node | Overkill for our scale |
| **Setup time** | Days (uses existing Redis, same library) | Weeks (provisioning, tuning, new client lib) |
| **Ops burden** | None new — already run Redis | New JVM cluster, ZooKeeper/KRaft, new failure modes |
| **Consumer groups** | Native via XREADGROUP/XPENDING/XCLAIM | Native via consumer groups |
| **Exactly-once (billing)** | Consumer-side idempotency (UUID + PG unique constraint) | Kafka EOS still needs same consumer-side pattern |
| **WebSocket push** | Redis Pub/Sub bridge — same ecosystem | Needs separate bridge service |
| **Team fit** | Already known; no learning curve | No Kafka experience on a 6-person team |

The ADR also documents the concrete retry/redelivery mechanism (`XCLAIM` with re-enqueue to a delayed stream), the billing exactly-once approach (producer-generated event ID + PostgreSQL unique constraint as the final dedup authority), and explicit trigger conditions for when to revisit this decision (sustained throughput >100k msg/s, need for >30-day retention, or dedicated infra engineer joining the team).
